    <h2>Letter of Credit (LC)</h2>
    <p>An LC is issued by your bank and guarantees payment to us once we present the agreed shipping documents. It's the most secure option for both sides on larger or first-time orders, since payment is conditional on document compliance rather than trust alone. We typically work with an irrevocable LC at sight; usance terms can be discussed for established relationships.</p>

    <h2>Advance payment / Cash Against Documents (CAD)</h2>
    <p>A deposit is paid to confirm the order, with the balance paid against shipping documents (often through the buyer's and our banks, document-for-payment). The advance percentage and document-release terms are agreed per order — this is common for trial orders and repeat buyers with an established relationship.</p>

    <h2>Telegraphic Transfer (TT)</h2>
    <p>A direct bank transfer. Terms vary — some orders use TT in advance for smaller trial quantities; others use a split (partial advance, balance before or after shipment) once trust is established between both parties.</p>

    <h2>Which should I choose?</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Situation</th><th scope="col">Typically suits</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>First order, larger value</td><td>LC</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Trial order, smaller value</td><td>TT or CAD</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Established, repeat relationship</td><td>TT or CAD, sometimes with more flexible splits</td></tr>
        </tbody>
